YANI: original equipment improves with age

[really, I'd guess it is pretty likely that
variants of this idea have been proposed
before]

This idea is spun off from the various IRC YANIs
being currently discussed.

To satisfy the often repeated desire to further
differentiate the classes for the entire game, I
propose that "original equipment" have a special
status: it improves _slowly_ with age, but only
while it is be]ing carried by the adventurer.

Armor: slowly gains various bonuses, AC improves,
fooproofing crosses a greyscale zone of "probable
restance to fooing"; MR/MC cross a greyscale zone
instead of a four three step scale; helms grow more
brilliant, gloves grow more dexterous, boots grow
more stealthy, kickier, more floatworthy.

Tools: as the PC practices their use, do their tool
stuff faster or better or more reliably; tinning
kits produce less rotted tins, lights cover vaster
areas, leashes keep pets more under control, pick
axes dig faster, drums grow scarier, horns grow more
plentiful, ...

Weapons: as the PC idly hones and polishes them,
grow sharper or blunter as appropriate, maces grow
bludgeonier, swords grow beheadingier or
bisectingier or can be swung more often or to better
effect, whips steal better, daggers hit harder,
dwarven mattocks grow lighter, ...

Food: ripens and ages as it is carried, grows more
nourishing, more "trail-ready", less rot-susceptible.

Spellbooks: grow easier to read, educate the PC for
longer, provide buffer spell effects, ...

Potions: warm against the PC's flesh, absorb
emotions and sweat, increase chances across a
greyscale of having enhanced good effects or
lessened bad effects.

Gems harden, as the PC absentmindedly polishes them,
grow less likely to be glasslike when IDed, gain
wisdom and intelligence until scary things happen.

You get the idea.

The biggest part of this is that binary or integer
steps like enchantments would be replaced by "as
smooth as implementable" _probabilities_ of the item
performing better at each point where its employment
happens, and these probabilities would improve with
time.

The intent of all this is that PCs would have
(possibly persuasive) reasons to cart around and use
their original equipment for the entire game, making
difficult choices about replacement equipment that
might be "better now", but over time..., and since
that original equipment is role specific, roles
would stay more distinguished over the course of the
game.

Comments?

